{%- comment -%}
  fye-related-products — the "you may also like" row on a product page.

  Built 01/09/2026. Ed's relationship metafield is being generated now, so this
  is written to read it the moment it lands AND to render something sensible
  until then — the section is designed and reviewable today rather than blocked
  on the data.

  ── WHERE THE RELATIONSHIPS COME FROM ─────────────────────────────────────

  Shopify's own Search & Discovery fields, which are already DEFINED on this
  store (verified 31/08/2026) and being populated now:

    shopify--discovery--product_recommendation.related_products
    shopify--discovery--product_recommendation.complementary_products

  Both are `list.product_reference`, so `.value` is a list of product drops —
  no handle lookups, no all_products cost, and a renamed product follows
  automatically.

  **THE NAMESPACE MUST BE READ WITH BRACKETS.** It contains hyphens, which
  Liquid cannot parse in dot notation:

      product.metafields.shopify--discovery--product_recommendation   ✗ nil
      product.metafields['shopify--discovery--product_recommendation'] ✓

  The dotted form does not error. It silently returns nil, so the section
  quietly falls back and looks like the data never arrived. Worth knowing
  before anyone "tidies" this.

  RELATED vs COMPLEMENTARY is a `source` setting rather than two sections,
  because the markup is identical and the difference is entirely editorial:

    related        other rings to consider INSTEAD of this one
    complementary  what goes WITH it — the matching band

  So the same section is added twice to a template with different settings and
  different wording. Shopify's two fields mean the same distinction, which is
  why they are kept apart rather than merged into one row.

  ── THE FALLBACK ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  A product with no relationships set shows its collection siblings instead —
  server-rendered, no JS, no recommendations API call. Two reasons this is not
  just a stopgap:

    · most of the catalogue will never have hand-picked relationships, and a
      collection sibling is a genuinely good suggestion for a ring;
    · it uses the SAME collection the breadcrumb chose (the route the shopper
      actually took, else the product's first real collection), so the trail
      and the row agree about where this ring lives.

  Set `fallback` to false to make the section disappear when there is no
  curated list — appropriate for a complementary row, where a guess is worse
  than silence: "buy this with it" has to be a real pairing.

  ── WHY IT MAY RENDER NOTHING ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  Deliberately, in these cases:

    · fewer than two items survive filtering. A "related products" row of one
      is not a row — it is a non-sequitur next to a 4-column grid.
    · every reference points at the current product, a deleted product or a
      draft. product_reference keeps pointing at products that have been
      deleted, so the nil guard below is load-bearing.

  ── PARAMETERS THIS DOES NOT NEED ─────────────────────────────────────────

  No card markup, no heading markup. `product-card` and `sect-head` already
  exist and are used by the collection page, so a ring in this row is
  pixel-identical to the same ring in a grid — which is the point of them being
  snippets (see product-card's own comment: it was written for this).
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign s = section.settings
  assign p = product

  assign ns = 'shopify--discovery--product_recommendation'
  assign key = 'related_products'
  if s.source == 'complementary'
    assign key = 'complementary_products'
  endif

  assign curated = p.metafields[ns][key].value

  comment
    Build the list. Curated first; the collection fallback only runs when the
    curated list yields nothing usable, never to top up a short list — a
    hand-picked pair of rings followed by two arbitrary siblings reads as
    though nobody chose any of them.
  endcomment
  assign picks = ''
  assign found = 0

  if curated != blank
    for item in curated
      if found >= s.count
        break
      endif
      unless item == blank or item.handle == p.handle or item.available == false
        assign picks = picks | append: item.handle | append: ','
        assign found = found | plus: 1
      endunless
    endfor
  endif

  comment
    Fallback: siblings from the same collection the breadcrumb chose.
  endcomment
  assign fb_col = nil
  if found == 0 and s.fallback
    assign fb_col = collection
    unless fb_col
      for c in p.collections
        unless c.handle == 'all'
          assign fb_col = c
          break
        endunless
      endfor
    endunless

    if fb_col
      for item in fb_col.products
        if found >= s.count
          break
        endif
        unless item.handle == p.handle or item.available == false
          assign picks = picks | append: item.handle | append: ','
          assign found = found | plus: 1
        endunless
      endfor
    endif
  endif

  assign handles = picks | split: ','

  comment
    Two is the floor — see the file comment.
  endcomment
  assign show = false
  if handles.size >= 2
    assign show = true
  endif

  comment
    The heading. A curated row and a fallback row are not making the same
    claim, so they are allowed different wording: "We also recommend" is a
    promise the fallback cannot keep.
  endcomment
  assign head = s.heading
  if fb_col and s.heading_fallback != blank
    assign head = s.heading_fallback
  endif
-%}

{%- if show -%}
  <section class="band band--{{ s.ground }} rel" data-screen-label="Related products">
    <div class="wrap">
      {%- render 'sect-head',
            show: true,
            eyebrow: s.eyebrow,
            heading: head,
            intro: s.intro,
            align: s.align,
            flank: s.flank -%}

      <div class="rel__grid">
        {%- for handle in handles -%}
          {%- assign item = all_products[handle] -%}
          {%- if item != blank -%}
            {%- render 'product-card',
                  product: item,
                  loading: 'lazy',
                  sizes: '(max-width: 900px) 50vw, 25vw' -%}
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endfor -%}
      </div>

      {%- if s.link_label != blank and s.link_url != blank -%}
        <div class="rel__more">
          <a class="btn btn--outline" href="{{ s.link_url }}">{{ s.link_label }}</a>
        </div>
      {%- endif -%}
    </div>
  </section>
{%- endif -%}

{% stylesheet %}
/* Four across, matching the collection grid exactly, so the same ring is the
   same size in both places. A short row leaves the trailing columns empty
   rather than stretching its cards — a 3-card row at 1/3 width would read as
   a different, more important component. */
.fye .rel__grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  gap: var(--s7) var(--s5);
}

/* The row sits closer to the section head than a collection grid does: it is
   a suggestion, not the page's subject. */
.fye .rel .sect-head {
  margin-bottom: var(--s7);
}

.fye .rel__more {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  margin-top: var(--s7);
}

@media (max-width: 900px) {
  /* Two across, not three: at this width three cards put the ring images
     below the size where a setting is legible. */
  .fye .rel__grid {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s5) var(--s4);
  }
}
{% endstylesheet %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "Related products",
  "tag": "section",
  "class": "fye-related-products",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Reads the product's Search & Discovery relationships. Add this section twice — once for related rings, once for complementary — with different wording."
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "source",
      "label": "Relationship",
      "options": [
        { "value": "related", "label": "Related products — other rings to consider" },
        { "value": "complementary", "label": "Complementary products — what goes with it" }
      ],
      "default": "related"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "fallback",
      "label": "Fall back to collection siblings",
      "info": "When this product has no relationships set. Leave off for a complementary row, where a guessed pairing is worse than none.",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "count",
      "label": "How many to show",
      "min": 2,
      "max": 8,
      "step": 1,
      "default": 4,
      "info": "The row hides itself entirely if fewer than two survive."
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Wording" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "eyebrow", "label": "Eyebrow", "default": "You may also like" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "heading", "label": "Heading", "default": "We also recommend" },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "heading_fallback",
      "label": "Heading when falling back",
      "default": "More from this collection",
      "info": "Used when the row is showing collection siblings rather than chosen relationships."
    },
    { "type": "textarea", "id": "intro", "label": "Intro" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "align",
      "label": "Alignment",
      "options": [
        { "value": "center", "label": "Centred" },
        { "value": "left", "label": "Left" }
      ],
      "default": "center"
    },
    { "type": "checkbox", "id": "flank", "label": "Hairlines either side of the heading", "default": true },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Band" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "ground",
      "label": "Background",
      "options": [
        { "value": "white", "label": "White" },
        { "value": "ivory", "label": "Champagne ivory" }
      ],
      "default": "ivory",
      "info": "The product page above is white, so ivory keeps the band rhythm."
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "Optional link" },
    { "type": "text", "id": "link_label", "label": "Button label" },
    { "type": "url", "id": "link_url", "label": "Button link" }
  ],
  "presets": [{ "name": "Related products" }]
}
{% endschema %}
